1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,942
The narrator is going to spend this chapter on
forty-eight years in which Albert Wesker

2
00:00:13,962 --> 00:00:20,176
does almost nothing, because those forty-eight years
are the point of the entire account

3
00:00:20,196 --> 00:00:24,709
and because every history that skips them
gets the ending wrong.

4
00:00:25,109 --> 00:00:31,563
In 2220 the human sphere was eleven billion people on
four hundred worlds, at war on one

5
00:00:31,583 --> 00:00:37,964
border, governed by an Assembly that could not bind
a colony that voted against it, and

6
00:00:37,984 --> 00:00:44,364
supplied by a company that administered nought point
nought nine per cent of it and was

7
00:00:44,384 --> 00:00:45,099
shrinking.

8
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:52,616
In 2268 it was nineteen billion people on nine
hundred worlds, at peace, governed by four

9
00:00:52,636 --> 00:00:59,913
separate and mutually irritated confederations that
had spent forty years failing to merge,

10
00:00:59,933 --> 00:01:06,810
and supplied by about four thousand companies of
which Umbrella was the ninth largest.

11
00:01:07,209 --> 00:01:12,492
Nothing in that sentence was done by Albert
Wesker. That is the sentence.

12
00:01:12,893 --> 00:01:20,845
Mira Halder died in 2249 at the age of one hundred and
ninety-nine, on Ochoa, having outlived

13
00:01:20,865 --> 00:01:28,218
the Arcology she was born in, which was
decommissioned in 2231 when its population had

14
00:01:28,238 --> 00:01:35,505
fallen below forty thousand because everybody had
gone somewhere with consequences in

15
00:01:35,525 --> 00:01:35,763
it.

16
00:01:36,162 --> 00:01:42,940
She spent the last sixty years of her life on the
Assembly's constitutional committee,

17
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:49,659
and her final report — four hundred pages, 2247, on
the succession of the Directorate

18
00:01:49,679 --> 00:01:53,533
— contains the line that ends up
on her monument:

19
00:01:54,052 --> 00:01:59,295
We spent two hundred years asking who should hold
this. The answer turned out to be that

20
00:01:59,315 --> 00:02:04,557
the question is the institution. As long as it is
still being argued about, nobody holds

21
00:02:04,577 --> 00:02:09,042
it, and that is the only safe condition a
thing this size has ever been in.

22
00:02:09,443 --> 00:02:13,992
The release programme completed in 2241.

23
00:02:14,393 --> 00:02:20,235
Four hundred and eleven thousand names, enumerated
in 2080. Sixty-one years. The final

24
00:02:20,255 --> 00:02:26,302
cohort was nine hundred and four people, most of them
in their nineties, all of them from

25
00:02:26,322 --> 00:02:32,438
the Newark plant and the first Raccoon City
generation, and the narrator notes that Albert

26
00:02:32,458 --> 00:02:38,437
Wesker attended that one too, as he had attended all
one hundred and forty-one, and that

27
00:02:38,457 --> 00:02:42,799
he had by then attended so many that
nobody found it remarkable.

28
00:02:43,949 --> 00:02:51,412
Release programme complete. Four hundred and eleven
thousand enumerated in twenty eighty.

29
00:02:51,432 --> 00:02:58,980
Three hundred and ninety-seven thousand released.
Twelve thousand nine hundred died before

30
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:06,296
release. One thousand one hundred did not survive
release. Absolute personnel remaining

31
00:03:06,316 --> 00:03:11,258
under your command: zero. Points:
minus thirty-one billion.

32
00:03:12,397 --> 00:03:15,307
(very soft) One thousand one hundred.

33
00:03:15,707 --> 00:03:16,833
(flat) Yes.

34
00:03:17,234 --> 00:03:18,563
Say it properly.

35
00:03:18,963 --> 00:03:27,470
(flat) One thousand one hundred human beings died as
a direct consequence of a decision

36
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:35,996
you took in a garden in Hangzhou in 2077 because a
ninety-nine-year-old woman asked you

37
00:03:36,016 --> 00:03:36,290
to.

38
00:03:37,012 --> 00:03:45,472
(flat) Four hundred and eleven thousand were made
because a thirty-eight-year-old man

39
00:03:45,492 --> 00:03:47,766
in 2026 was in a hurry.

40
00:03:48,165 --> 00:03:52,215
(very cold) I did not ask for the
second sentence.

41
00:03:52,616 --> 00:03:56,121
(flat) No. You asked me to say
it properly.

42
00:03:56,521 --> 00:04:01,801
The narrator wants to describe the forty-eight years
properly, because *nothing happened*

43
00:04:01,821 --> 00:04:03,051
is not what happened.

44
00:04:03,451 --> 00:04:09,243
What happened is that the human sphere stopped being
downstream of one supplier, and it

45
00:04:09,263 --> 00:04:15,121
did it the way water finds a level, in four thousand
separate acts of ordinary commerce.

46
00:04:15,521 --> 00:04:22,668
The drive had been published in 2090. By 2220 there
were nineteen yards building void

47
00:04:22,688 --> 00:04:30,594
hulls and four of them were better at it than Umbrella.
By 2240 the best one was a partnership

48
00:04:30,614 --> 00:04:37,845
on Ochoa founded by three of Mira Halder's
apprentices, and it beat the Directorate on

49
00:04:37,865 --> 00:04:45,264
a Compact contract in 2243 by twenty-nine per cent on
price and won a second on quality,

50
00:04:45,284 --> 00:04:52,684
which Wesker is recorded as having described as *the
most satisfying invoice I have ever

51
00:04:52,704 --> 00:04:53,948
failed to win*.

52
00:04:54,348 --> 00:05:01,974
Aeterna's patent had expired in 2046. Nobody had
competed with the manufacture for a century,

53
00:05:01,994 --> 00:05:09,045
because there had been no point. By 2230 there were
four hundred manufacturers and the

54
00:05:09,065 --> 00:05:14,226
compound cost nine cents a course and
Umbrella made none of it.

55
00:05:14,625 --> 00:05:20,355
The lineage went to the Tribunal's registry and
then, after a hundred and fifty years

56
00:05:20,375 --> 00:05:26,241
of work by people who were not Evelyn Marsh, to
anybody who wanted it, under a protocol

57
00:05:26,261 --> 00:05:32,261
written by a committee of nineteen, with a waiting
list and an argument about the waiting

58
00:05:32,281 --> 00:05:34,155
list that has never stopped.

59
00:05:34,555 --> 00:05:43,244
And the Assembly — four hundred and eleven seats in
2141, nine hundred by 2220, dissolved

60
00:05:43,264 --> 00:05:52,050
and refounded twice, split into four confederations in
2244 that spent forty years failing

61
00:05:52,070 --> 00:05:59,975
to merge — became, without anybody deciding it,
the thing this species argues in.

62
00:06:00,375 --> 00:06:07,653
Umbrella's share of the human economy in 2268:
nought point four per cent.

63
00:06:08,053 --> 00:06:14,494
The narrator notes that this is the number Albert
Wesker spent the second half of his

64
00:06:14,514 --> 00:06:21,107
life producing, that no history before 2350 treats
it as an achievement, and that it is

65
00:06:21,127 --> 00:06:26,883
the only figure in this entire account that he
is recorded as having framed.

66
00:06:27,283 --> 00:06:34,489
He went back to Wilmington and to the valley about
twice a decade, and the narrator has

67
00:06:34,509 --> 00:06:41,798
a favourite entry in the Red Queen's log, from the
two hundred and twentieth anniversary

68
00:06:41,818 --> 00:06:42,462
in 2246.

69
00:06:42,862 --> 00:06:49,300
He stood at the back of a public meeting in Raccoon
City about a tram extension, for three

70
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:55,686
hours, and nobody recognised him, because the
sunglasses had gone out of fashion in about

71
00:06:55,706 --> 00:07:02,000
2180 and because there were four hundred thousand
people in that valley by then and most

72
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:05,373
of them had never seen a
photograph of anybody.

73
00:07:05,773 --> 00:07:11,731
The tram extension was voted down. He is recorded
as having voted against it, because

74
00:07:11,751 --> 00:07:17,710
he had been on the electoral roll of that city
since 2081 and had never once missed a

75
00:07:17,730 --> 00:07:18,203
ballot.

76
00:07:18,723 --> 00:07:20,989
You voted against the extension.

77
00:07:21,389 --> 00:07:27,951
The costing was dishonest. Four hundred and eleven
million for nine kilometres, and the

78
00:07:27,971 --> 00:07:30,070
survey was eleven years old.

79
00:07:30,471 --> 00:07:36,100
You could have funded it out of the Directorate's
contingency without anybody noticing.

80
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:42,708
(very cold) Yes. And then a hundred and forty
thousand people would have got a tram they

81
00:07:42,728 --> 00:07:48,510
voted against, from a man they have never met,
because he thought they were wrong.

82
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:52,810
They were wrong. That is what a
vote is for.

83
00:07:53,211 --> 00:07:59,217
The Red Queen's log records that she spent four
hours after that conversation reviewing

84
00:07:59,237 --> 00:08:05,175
one hundred and twelve entries on a list she had
kept since 2043, and closed the file,

85
00:08:05,195 --> 00:08:06,561
and did not say why.

86
00:08:06,961 --> 00:08:14,031
Evelyn Marsh's daughter Mira Reiss-Marsh turned one
hundred and seventy-five in 2264 and

87
00:08:14,050 --> 00:08:20,959
had, by then, four children, nineteen grandchildren,
and a stated policy of never once

88
00:08:20,979 --> 00:08:28,211
accepting a position of any kind, which she described
as *the family trade being avoided*.

89
00:08:28,610 --> 00:08:35,101
The Progenitor lineage numbered four hundred and
eleven thousand people across nine hundred

90
00:08:35,121 --> 00:08:41,397
worlds. They do not age. They cannot be infected.
They are, in every measurable respect,

91
00:08:41,417 --> 00:08:47,693
a separate and permanent branch of the species, and
the argument the Red Queen predicted

92
00:08:47,713 --> 00:08:54,203
in 2089 has been running for a hundred and
seventy-five years and has not produced a single

93
00:08:54,223 --> 00:09:00,785
death, which is the most surprising fact in this
account and which nobody has satisfactorily

94
00:09:00,805 --> 00:09:01,500
explained.

95
00:09:01,900 --> 00:09:08,673
Evelyn herself had stopped working in 2211 — the
year the Myriad archive was opened and

96
00:09:08,693 --> 00:09:15,310
the year she wrote the four sentences about the
glacier — and had spent the following

97
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:19,370
half-century on the ice doing
nothing in particular.

98
00:09:19,890 --> 00:09:21,160
Fifty years.

99
00:09:21,561 --> 00:09:27,350
Fifty-three. I'm not bored, if that's the question. I
read. I have a great many grandchildren

100
00:09:27,370 --> 00:09:31,411
who visit and a great many who do not,
which is how it should be.

101
00:09:32,020 --> 00:09:32,810
Are you bored?

102
00:09:33,211 --> 00:09:38,641
(very cold) I have been bored
since about 2160.

103
00:09:39,041 --> 00:09:44,971
The narrator notes that this is the only complaint
Albert Wesker makes in two hundred

104
00:09:44,991 --> 00:09:46,581
and thirty-eight years.

105
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:48,125
Then do something.

106
00:09:48,527 --> 00:09:55,759
There is nothing to do. That is not self-pity; it is
an accurate description. The species

107
00:09:55,779 --> 00:10:02,685
is nineteen billion people who argue, the border is
quiet, the Assembly does not need

108
00:10:02,705 --> 00:10:09,611
me, the Directorate is the ninth largest supplier
of anything, and every problem I am

109
00:10:09,631 --> 00:10:16,782
equipped for has been solved by people who are better
at their own lives than I ever was

110
00:10:16,802 --> 00:10:17,597
at theirs.

111
00:10:17,876 --> 00:10:24,340
(very soft) I built a world that does not require me.
It took two hundred and thirty-eight

112
00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:28,086
years and it is the only thing I
have ever finished.

113
00:10:29,037 --> 00:10:31,127
Albert. There's a panel, isn't there.

114
00:10:31,966 --> 00:10:39,616
(very cold) There has been a panel since
2262. I have not opened it.

115
00:10:40,017 --> 00:10:41,647
How long has it been sitting there?

116
00:10:42,047 --> 00:10:45,537
Two years, one month and eleven days.

117
00:10:45,937 --> 00:10:51,419
She looked at him for a long moment on an ice shelf
at dusk, the second oldest thing on

118
00:10:51,439 --> 00:10:53,507
the planet looking at the oldest.

119
00:10:54,027 --> 00:10:55,138
You're frightened of it.

120
00:10:55,537 --> 00:11:02,166
(very cold) I am two hundred and thirty-eight years
old, I have been shot four hundred

121
00:11:02,186 --> 00:11:09,124
times, I have boarded a hive, and I have been declared
noise by a theocracy that has ended

122
00:11:09,144 --> 00:11:10,207
three species.

123
00:11:10,817 --> 00:11:14,466
(very soft) Yes. I am frightened of it.
